## Evidence

### NFPA 72 speech intelligibility

NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code) requires that emergency
communication systems achieve, in each acoustically distinguishable space, an
average Speech Transmission Index of ≥ 0.50 with ≥ 90 percent of measurement
points ≥ 0.45; equivalent to 0.70 on the Common Intelligibility Scale. This
is the code floor for any voice-evacuation or mass-notification loudspeaker,
including in airport terminals [Source:
https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]. NFPA 72 Chapter 24
defines four layers of emergency communication — indoor, outdoor, personal
notification (text/email), and public (radio/TV) — and requires that each
mass-notification application be specific to the risks of the facility, which
maps directly to airport terminal design [Source:
https://prod-edam.honeywell.com/content/dam/honeywell-edam/hbt/en-us/documents/literature-and-specs/whitepapers/notifier-us/hon-ba-fire-nf-wp-massnotification-m-wp-mn-05-10.pdf].
An intelligibility measurement discipline is documented in the Eaton white
paper on the NFPA 72 audibility-vs-intelligibility distinction [Source:
https://www.eaton.com/content/dam/eaton/products/safety-security-emergency-communications/lsmns/waves/documents/lsmns-intelligibility-white-paper.pdf].

### NFPA 415 — airport terminal buildings

NFPA 415 sets the minimum fire-protection requirements for airport terminal
buildings, fueling ramp drainage, and loading walkways, including exit
capacity for peak-holiday-occupancy conditions and smoke-removal in large-
volume spaces where sprinkler protection alone is insufficient [Source:
https://firelogai.com/blog/fire-protection-airports-aviation-terminals-nfpa-415].
Current editions include 2016, 2022, and 2026; MWAA design manuals must
name the edition applied, because peak-occupancy egress and smoke-removal
assumptions change edition-to-edition [Source:
https://www.fire-police-ems.com/NFPA415-2026.shtml].

### 14 CFR Part 382 Subpart D — accessibility of airport facilities

Air-carrier obligations under Part 382 Subpart D require carriers to make
terminal facilities they own, lease, or control accessible for individuals
with disabilities, with information provided for flight safety, ticketing,
check-in, delays, cancellations, schedule changes, boarding, connections,
gate assignments, baggage, oversales, paging, aircraft changes affecting
persons with disabilities, and emergencies [Source:
https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382/subpart-D].

### Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International Airport, 6 Jan 2017 — after-action

The Police Executive Research Institute after-action report on the FLL
active-shooter incident documents that staging areas were established "on
the fly" and never broadcast to responding units, and that the Mobile
Command Vehicle was placed in a location that rendered its communications
capability useless [Source:
https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf].
Follow-on coverage documents that FLL subsequently obtained access to
IPAWS (Integrated Public Alert and Warning System) as a post-incident
corrective action; before the shooting, the airport had no direct authority
to push emergency messaging to passengers evacuating onto the ramp
[Source:
https://airportimprovement.com/article/fort-lauderdale-int-l-shares-lessons-learned-2017-active-shooter-tragedy/].

### TSA and public-area security post-FLL

Under the 2018 TSA Modernization Act, TSA and CISA established the Public
Area Security Working Group; TSA subsequently mandated active-shooter
training drills, installed duress alarms at screening checkpoints, and
revised directives on local law enforcement coverage of passenger screening
checkpoints and adjacent public areas [Source:
https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-278]. TSA's Public Area Security
National Framework and its 2019 Best Practices update document the current
federal expectation for airport-operator response to soft-target attacks
[Source: https://www.tsa.gov/sites/default/files/pass_national_framework.pdf].

### OSHA occupational noise (29 CFR 1910.95)

OSHA's occupational-noise standard requires a hearing-conservation program
at an 8-hour TWA of 85 dBA (action level); engineering or administrative
controls become mandatory before PPE reliance at 90 dBA TWA (permissible
exposure limit) [Source: https://www.osha.gov/noise]. Airport ramp and
gate-adjacent environments regularly exceed the action level, with peak
impulse events well above 115 dBA [Source:
https://hearingprotect.com/en/blog/hearing-protection-aviation-airport-ground-crews-guide/].

### Wireless 911 location routing (FCC)

The FCC's 2024 Report and Order on Facilitating Implementation of Next
Generation 911 Services and Location-Based Routing for Wireless 911 Calls
proposed that CMRS providers use location-based routing when horizontal-
location accuracy meets specified thresholds (approximately 165 meters at
90 percent confidence); the Commission noted at the time that most 911
delivery networks did not yet have the ability to provide geospatial
routing and that timing for universal deployment remained unknown
[Source:
https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/09/24/2024-18603/facilitating-implementation-of-next-generation-911-services-ng911-location-based-routing-for]
[Source:
https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/03/13/2024-03157/location-based-routing-for-wireless-911-calls].

### MWAA police jurisdiction and dispatch

MWAA operates a sworn police department with Virginia state certification
and concurrent jurisdiction in Loudoun, Fairfax, and Arlington Counties;
the county law enforcement agencies retain concurrent jurisdiction over
Authority facilities within their boundaries [Source:
https://www.mwaa.com/policefireems/mwaa-police-department]. MWAA's police
authority derives from § 5.1-158 of the Code of Virginia and related
provisions governing the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority
[Source: https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title5.1/chapter10/section5.1-158/].

### Schiphol as the operative "silent airport" reference

Schiphol publicly describes itself as a silent airport with virtually no
announcements, with information delivered through FIDS and the airport
app, and with PA reserved for weather-driven delays and genuine
emergencies [Source: https://www.vanemag.com/silent-airports-list-2026/].
The gap between that operating model and the FLL/DCA regulatory and
mutual-aid environment is the load-bearing caveat for MWAA: Schiphol
operates under Dutch fire and telecommunications codes, ICAO Annex 14
airport standards, and a hard-integrated national IPAWS-equivalent for
public alerting.

### DCA 29 Jan 2025 collision context

The January 29, 2025 midair collision between American Eagle Flight 5342
(CRJ-700 operated by PSA) and a US Army UH-60L on approach to DCA killed
67. All takeoffs and landings at DCA were halted; the terminal itself
remained open [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/Pages/DCA25MA108.aspx]
[Source: https://aviationweek.com/air-transport/safety-ops-regulation/no-survivors-after-midair-collision-american-airlines-crj-army].
The NTSB's January 2026 findings sharply criticized the FAA for helicopter
route design and for failing to analyze years of near-collision data
[Source: https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/27/us/ntsb-plane-crash-dc-potomac].
This is the political and operational backdrop against which any new DCA
terminal standard will be read.

