## The public safety case for and against the thesis

### For (the case for a mandatory quiet-by-design standard, from public safety)

- **Intelligibility rises with lower ambient.** The single largest
  contributor to failed STI measurements in existing airport terminals is
  background noise from concessions, TVs, unmanaged airline gate PA, and
  overlapping ceiling loudspeakers stepping on each other. A well-executed
  quiet standard — meaning specified NC target, RT60 range, PA zoning, and
  suppression of overlapping loudspeaker fields — usually raises STI. A
  fire-alarm voice-evacuation system in a quieter room is a better
  emergency notification system, not a worse one.
- **Zoned PA gives police and fire a scalpel.** A terminal-wide broadcast
  is unusable for a police shelter-in-place at one gate. Zoned
  loudspeakers, if governance is written correctly, let ARFF push
  evacuation to Concourse C without disturbing the security lines at the
  other end of the terminal.
- **Employee hearing exposure drops.** Quieter concourses reduce OSHA
  1910.95 exposure for MWAA and airline employees; over a 15-year Use &
  Lease that is real audiometric-testing, hearing-conservation, and
  workers'-comp cost.
- **Passenger attention to visual signage and emergency messaging
  increases.** In a lower-ambient environment, a visual alert on a FIDS or
  gate monitor is more likely to be seen. In a saturated announcement
  environment, passengers have learned to tune it all out — including the
  ones they need to hear.

### Against (the case against or in caution, from public safety)

- **Compliance is not competence.** A design that meets NFPA 72 on paper,
  in a Sunday-4-AM commissioning test, in an empty terminal, is not a
  system that meets NFPA 72 during a real Wednesday-5-PM Nor'easter with
  25,000 people in the concourse. Occupied-condition testing is expensive,
  disruptive, and almost never done. Without it, "quiet" hides
  intelligibility failures rather than exposes them.
- **The override path is where systems die.** A police officer, fire
  captain, or dispatcher needs to be able to seize any zone, silence
  airline gate PAs, and broadcast in under 15 seconds. Every additional
  zone, every additional tenant PA, every additional smart-audio device
  in a "quiet" terminal is an additional integration that must be tested
  and rehearsed. In the FLL 2017 timeline, response happened in seconds;
  in a distributed quiet-audio architecture, the override plumbing has
  more places to break.
- **The airline gate PA is the noise source and the airlines will not
  concede it easily.** If the standard cannot enter the airline gate-lease
  exhibits and the tenant standards in the MWAA Design Manual with
  enforceable audit, the airport will build a quiet terminal and the
  gates will remain loud. The 2025 Use & Lease was signed to a $9 B CCP
  without a quiet-standard exhibit; adding it now requires airline
  concurrence.
- **The accessibility, digital-exclusion, and irregular-ops floor is
  higher than the industry admits.** International "silent airport"
  models (Schiphol, Copenhagen, Helsinki, London City) operate on much
  higher smartphone penetration, much lower elderly and infrequent-flier
  populations, and different regulatory backstops. IAD's international
  arrivals include first-time US visitors from 100+ countries; DCA
  carries a heavy federal-employee and constituency-visitor mix. Both
  populations need a lower dependence on the app than "silent airport"
  practice assumes.
- **PSAP routing has not caught up.** Wireless 911 calls from inside an
  MWAA terminal do not reliably route to MWAA police dispatch first. The
  NG911 rulemaking will not solve this at every carrier, on every tower,
  by the time Concourse E opens. Building a quiet-terminal standard that
  assumes passengers will "just call 911" as a substitute for PA is
  building on unfinished federal infrastructure.

