## Operational realities a strategist could use

1. **Occupied-condition acceptance testing is the single lever that
   converts quiet-by-design from a compliance exercise into a real
   life-safety upgrade.** Write it into the MWAA Design Manual and the
   commissioning specifications for every terminal project — STI verified
   by an independent acoustician at 60 percent and 100 percent peak-hour
   occupancy, retested after each substantial tenant fit-out, with a
   corrective-action bond if the space fails post-opening. Nobody in US
   airport practice does this consistently. It costs six figures per
   concourse. It is a cheaper insurance policy than an after-action
   report.

2. **Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood, January 6, 2017: five killed, six shot,
   thirty-six injured in the follow-on ramp evacuation, 12,000+ passengers
   self-evacuated onto the AOA.** The after-action found that the airport
   had a good plan and bad execution because staging was ad-hoc, the MCV
   was sited where its radios did not work, and the airport was not
   authorized on IPAWS. MWAA should verify — and the run should state —
   that both airports are IPAWS-authorized, that the MWAA dispatch center
   has active WEA (Wireless Emergency Alert) origination, and that the
   quiet-by-design standard names WEA and IPAWS as required override
   channels, not just PA.

3. **NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 average and ≥ 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement
   points, in every acoustically distinguishable space, verified with a
   calibrated test source.** This is the single number the standard needs
   to name. It is the number a plaintiff's expert will name in a
   post-incident deposition. It is what an independent acoustician can
   test in an afternoon. It is what most airport commissioning skips.

4. **MWAA sworn officer authority derives from § 5.1-158 of the Code of
   Virginia with concurrent jurisdiction in Arlington, Fairfax, and
   Loudoun Counties.** MWAA can control the terminal-side response. It
   cannot control what a passenger's cellphone does when they dial 911.
   Any quiet-by-design standard should be accompanied by a documented
   PSAP-transfer test — pick up a phone on ten jet bridges across IAD and
   DCA, dial 911, and time and log where the call lands and how long the
   transfer to MWAA dispatch takes. If the answer is "Loudoun County ECC,
   90 seconds," write it in the brief and use it to fund the NG911
   integration in parallel.

5. **NFPA 415, 14 CFR Part 382 Subpart D, OSHA 1910.95, NFPA 72 Chapter
   24, and 14 CFR Part 139 emergency-notification obligations form the
   non-negotiable envelope.** A quiet-by-design standard is a subset of
   that envelope, not a substitute for it. The correct sequence in the
   MWAA design manual is: state the federal and code floor first
   (audibility, intelligibility, accessibility, egress, employee
   exposure), then layer quiet-by-design targets on top (ambient NC, RT60,
   zone spillover, announcement-frequency governance, tenant broadcast
   rights). Written in that order, a quiet standard is defensible; written
   in the reverse order, it invites the executive-review board to strike
   the quiet targets the first time the general counsel notices what the
   standard actually promises.

